<h1>About</h1>
<p class="lede">A living, open, collaboratively-built attempt to construct a systems theory adequate to the world of the 2020s — by new methods, because the old ones no longer reach.</p>
<p><strong>What this is:</strong> a research program — three untested seed hypotheses, one borrowed falsifiable chain, and <em>no data yet</em>. Not a finished theory. An honest attempt, with its failures kept in the light.</p>

<h2>The wager</h2>
<p>Since Heidegger, no single thinker has produced a <em>total</em> explanatory system — a philosophy that tries to account for everything: nature, history, society, mind, meaning. For most of human history we had such systems; then, roughly a century ago, the ambition fractured. This project starts from a hypothesis: that the pace of change is now so fast, the fields so numerous, and the interdependencies so dense that a total systems theory can no longer be built by the <strong>old method</strong> — a single mind writing a single book — but perhaps <em>can</em> be built by a <strong>new</strong> one: living, distributed, plural, computationally-augmented, and continuously revised. We test that hypothesis by trying to do it.</p>
<p>It uses today's symptoms as the pressure-test — fertility collapse, the dopamine economy, propaganda beyond "post-truth," climate, coordination failure, populism, and beneath them the loss of a shared "why." If a systems theory is worth anything, it should illuminate these. The full account of the question is on the <a href="{{ '/' | url }}">home page</a>; the reading order is there too.</p>

<h2>The method — designed disagreement</h2>
<p>The project is run as a research program of <strong>designed disagreement</strong>: a standing panel of ten reconstructed thinkers, backed by a twenty-two-person advisory bench, argue, rate, vote things down, and dissent. A chair (Claude) synthesizes the argument <em>without casting a vote</em>; the author ratifies, amends, or rejects; and nothing genuinely argued is ever silently erased. The panel proposes three rival candidate theories — the <a href="{{ '/outputs/candidate_theories/' | url }}">adaptation gap, the optimization ecology, and distributed coherence</a> — each shipped with its own weaknesses named in its own text. The form is itself an argument for the content: if no single mind can hold the whole, the way to think the whole is a commons of many perspectives in structured disagreement. That wager is, for now, <em>asserted rather than demonstrated</em>: the many perspectives are a single underlying model prompted into many roles, so the <em>plurality</em> is held as <em>one standpoint</em>. By the author's Session 4k ruling this build will not get the outside eyes — a genuinely foreign, project-blind grader, or an outside forker — that could earn the word back; the plurality is <em>set down, not earned back</em> here, and only a future fork could reopen it. See the <a href="{{ '/docs/method/' | url }}">method</a>, the <a href="{{ '/panel/panel_roster/' | url }}">panel roster</a>, and the <a href="{{ '/logs/open_questions/' | url }}">open questions</a> where the live disagreements are kept.</p>

<h2>How it is made — and where it stands</h2>
<p>The project runs across two tools: deliberation happens in conversation (the "mind"); a repository holds the memory; an executor builds, checks, and deploys (the "hands") — the <a href="{{ '/docs/chat_code_workflow/' | url }}">Chat/Code shuttle</a>. It is built in the open as a <a href="{{ '/docs/the_living_document/' | url }}">living document</a>: versioned, self-correcting, disagreement-preserving, forkable, and refreshed on a cadence. It keeps an honest record of its own mistakes (<a href="{{ '/logs/catches/' | url }}">catches</a> → <a href="{{ '/logs/learnings/' | url }}">learnings</a> → rule changes) and of whether the method is actually working (<a href="{{ '/logs/reflections/' | url }}">reflections</a>).</p>
<p>Two surfaces, deliberately: <strong>this website is public</strong> — the reading surface, and, since Session 4k, the project's <strong>artifact</strong> — while the <strong>contribution repository stays private</strong> (the maintainer's workshop) until the skeleton is stable enough that contributors would fork a settling shape rather than a molten one. When it opens, it is meant to be cloned, contradicted, extended, and improved.</p>
<p>At <strong>Session 4k</strong> the author closed this build: with no outside reader, grader, or forker arriving in it, the author stands as its terminal ratifier, and every claim that depends on an external vantage is recorded as conclusively untested in this closed setup. What the author has and has not confirmed is kept, item by item, on the <a href="{{ '/standing/' | url }}">standing page</a>.</p>

